-- title:
--   Invertibility of the section ideal for a section through a smooth open
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $C$ be a scheme and let $c \colon C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be separated. Let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $c$, that is, a morphism $\varepsilon \colon \operatorname{Spec} R \to C$ with $\varepsilon \circ c$ equal to the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} R$ (in diagrammatic order, $\varepsilon$ followed by $c$). Let $U \subseteq C$ be an open subscheme such that the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow C$ followed by $c$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$, and assume the set-theoretic image of $\varepsilon$ is contained in $U$. Then for every scheme $V$ and every morphism $u \colon V \to \operatorname{Spec} R$, the ideal sheaf datum `sectionIdeal c ε u` on the fibre product $C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} V$ — by definition the kernel of the induced section $\operatorname{rigSection}$, the morphism $V \to C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} V$ with components $u$ followed by $\varepsilon$ and $\mathrm{id}_V$ — is invertible in the following sense: every point of $C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} V$ lies in a basic open $D(f)$ of some affine open $W$ with $f \in \Gamma(W)$, such that on the affine basic open determined by $f$ the ideal is generated by a single non-zero-divisor of its ring of sections.
--
--   This is the statement that the section $\varepsilon_V \colon V \to C \times_R V$ cuts out a relative effective Cartier divisor, under the weaker hypothesis that only an open neighbourhood $U$ of the image of $\varepsilon$ — rather than the whole of $C$ — is smooth of relative dimension one over the base. It supplies the local-principality input for the rigidified line bundles and twisting constructions used in the treatment of the relative Picard functor, and is invoked by the results on relative effective Cartier divisors supported in a given locus and on Euler characteristics of the corresponding fibres.
